#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""btrfs-snapshot-core: local read-only snapshots with btrbk-compatible retention.

Installed by btrfs-setup.sh (CST) as /usr/local/sbin/btrfs-snapshot-core
and called by /usr/local/sbin/btrfs-snapshot (hourly, emergency purge, NAS
pinning). Replaces btrbk for LOCAL snapshots only. Stdlib only, runs as root.
Everything comes from the command line; it reads no configuration file.

  btrfs-snapshot-core snapshot --snapdir DIR --source NAME=PATH:POLICY [...]
                      [--dry-run] [--force]
      per source: creates SNAPDIR/NAME.YYYYMMDDTHHMM (read-only) if the source
      changed since its newest snapshot (or that one is older than 24 h, or
      --force), then deletes what POLICY no longer keeps.
  btrfs-snapshot-core prune --snapdir DIR --source NAME=PATH:POLICY [...]
                      [--dry-run] [--allow-mass-delete]
      deletions only (the emergency purge passes POLICY '24h 3d' and
      --allow-mass-delete).
  btrfs-snapshot-core list --snapdir DIR --source NAME=PATH:POLICY [...] [--json]
      every snapshot with the keep/delete decision and its reason.
  btrfs-snapshot-core latest --snapdir DIR --name NAME [--source-path PATH]
      prints the path of the newest snapshot of NAME (by "Gen at creation").

POLICY: like btrbk's snapshot_preserve, '<N>h <N>d <N>w <N>m <N>y' separated by
spaces or commas, '*' for "all"; e.g. '72h 7d 4w 3m'. The decisions are those of
btrbk 0.32.7 schedule() with preserve_hour_of_day 0, preserve_day_of_week
sunday and snapshot_preserve_min latest:
  * hours/days/weeks are counted on the local wall clock, from the start of the
    hour/day (00:00)/week (Sunday 00:00); a month or year starts on its first
    Sunday; "<N>h" keeps buckets 0..N (inclusive), likewise for d/w/m/y;
  * in every bucket the OLDEST snapshot is the one kept ("first of hour",
    "first of day" among the first-of-hour ones, "first weekly" ...);
  * the newest snapshot is always kept, and so is any dated in the future.
One known deviation: in zones whose DST shift is not a whole hour (only
Australia/Lord_Howe, 30 minutes) a name in the repeated half hour of the DST
end is read as the earlier instant, which is what localtime() maps back to that
name; btrbk's Time::Local returns an instant 30 minutes off there, so the two
can keep a different snapshot of that hour/month. Whole-hour zones (this
host's America/Sao_Paulo, Europe/*, America/*) match btrbk exactly.

Change detection: a source changed iff its Generation is greater than the
"Gen at creation" of its newest snapshot (newest by that generation, not by
name). Never "!=": since kernel 7.1 an idle source's generation stays BELOW
the generation its snapshot was created in (btrbk issue #656). A source's
Generation moves when the transaction holding a write commits (every 30 s or
at a sync), so a write in the last seconds before a run is picked up by the
next run. An unchanged source still gets a snapshot once its newest one is
older than 24 h (by the name), or when its newest one is dated in the future
while the clock is NTP-synchronized (see below).

Safeguards on top of the policy (a snapshot is deleted only if all agree):
  * ownership: only directory entries named NAME.YYYYMMDDTHHMM[_N] that are a
    real subvolume (inode 256, not a symlink), read-only, and whose Parent UUID
    is the source's UUID are considered at all; anything else is never deleted;
  * the 3 newest snapshots of a source and any younger than 24 h are kept, and
    so is every path listed in the pin file (one per line; the NAS copy pins
    what it is reading);
  * clock guards for deletions: none while the clock is not NTP-synchronized,
    or is behind the newest snapshot (by "Gen at creation") or the last
    successful run (minus 5 minutes) - a name from the wrong time would put
    the snapshot in the wrong retention bucket;
  * circuit breaker: a normal run that would delete more than max(5, 10% of the
    owned) snapshots of a source deletes none of them (exit 4) unless
    --allow-mass-delete. Deletions the passing of time explains do not count:
    a snapshot the policy still kept at the last successful run (state file)
    and that has aged out since is expected, however long the machine was off.
    What counts is everything else - a shrunken policy, snapshots with old
    names that appeared since, or the wall clock having moved further than
    the real time elapsed since a last run in the same boot (CLOCK_BOOTTIME,
    which includes suspend). With no recorded run every deletion counts. When
    it trips: check with `list`, then run `prune ... --allow-mass-delete`
    (--list-hint/--confirm-hint name a caller's own commands in the message);
  * the directory is listed again right before deleting and every candidate is
    re-checked (same UUID, still owned, still due); deletions go one by one.

Clock guards for creation (a snapshot's name is its time, and a wrong name
outlives the clock error):
  * no snapshot while the clock is behind the last successful run (minus 5
    minutes) - the state file is removed by hand if the clock is right;
  * not NTP-synchronized: no snapshot if the clock is behind the newest
    snapshot, or moved further than the real time elapsed since a last run in
    this boot, or - in the first 30 minutes after boot - is more than 24 h
    ahead of both the newest snapshot and the last successful run (the catch-up
    run of a Persistent timer can start before chrony has stepped a wrong RTC;
    the next run catches up). Otherwise (an offline machine) the snapshot is
    taken with the unverified time and a notice;
  * NTP-synchronized but the newest snapshot is dated in the future (it was
    named while the clock ran ahead): a new, correctly named snapshot is taken
    and a warning names the old one. It becomes the newest by "Gen at
    creation", so deletions resume; the future-dated one is kept ("preserve
    forced") until its date passes, or deleted by hand with `btrfs subvolume
    delete` after checking `list`.
The state file (--state-dir/core-last-run: epoch, boot id, CLOCK_BOOTTIME) is
written only by successful runs with an NTP-synchronized clock, and never
moves backwards.

Locking: snapshot and prune take an exclusive, non-blocking flock on --lock
(default /run/btrfs-snapshot.lock, the same file btrfs-snapshot locks for its
hourly run and the conversions, so manual runs exclude both) and exit 3 if it
is busy. A caller that already holds that lock (btrfs-snapshot hourly) passes
--no-lock: flock locks belong to an open file description, so a second open of
the same file from the child would conflict with the parent's lock. list and
latest take no lock.

Exit codes: 0 ok, 1 error, 2 usage, 3 lock busy, 4 a safety check blocked a
creation or deletion (the log says which and why).
Log lines go to stdout with the journald '<N>btrfs-snapshot: ' prefix (stderr
for `latest` and `list --json`, whose stdout is the result); one summary line
per snapshot/prune run.
"""

import argparse
import dataclasses
import datetime as dt
import fcntl
import json
import os
import re
import stat
import subprocess
import sys
import time
import zoneinfo

EXIT_OK, EXIT_ERROR, EXIT_USAGE, EXIT_BUSY, EXIT_REFUSED = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4

DEFAULT_LOCK = "/run/btrfs-snapshot.lock"
DEFAULT_LIST_HINT = "btrfs-snapshot-core list --snapdir DIR --source NAME=PATH:POLICY ..."
DEFAULT_CONFIRM_HINT = "btrfs-snapshot-core prune --snapdir DIR --source NAME=PATH:POLICY ... --allow-mass-delete"
DEFAULT_PIN_FILE = "/run/btrfs-snapshot/pinned"
DEFAULT_STATE_DIR = "/var/lib/btrfs-snapshot"
STATE_FILE = "core-last-run"       # "EPOCH BOOT_ID BOOTTIME" of the last successful synchronized run
BOOT_ID_FILE = "/proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id"

TS_FORMAT = "%Y%m%dT%H%M"          # btrbk timestamp_format long, local time
NAME_RE = re.compile(r"[A-Za-z0-9._-]+")
UUID_RE = re.compile(r"[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{12}")

KEEP_NEWEST = 3                    # never delete the 3 newest snapshots of a source
MIN_AGE = 24 * 3600                # ... nor any younger than this
SAFETY_NET = 24 * 3600             # snapshot an unchanged source at least this often
CLOCK_SLACK = 5 * 60               # tolerated clock step backwards (NTP slews, RTC drift)
NTP_GRACE = 30 * 60                # after boot: wait this long for NTP before trusting a far-ahead clock
MASS_DELETE_MIN = 5                # circuit breaker: more than max(5, 10% of owned)
MASS_DELETE_PERCENT = 10
CMD_TIMEOUT = 600

# Policy letters in btrbk's order; '*' is stored as "all".
POLICY_UNITS = "hdwmy"
POLICY_TOKEN = re.compile(r"(\*|[0-9]+)([hdwmy])", re.IGNORECASE)
DAY_NAMES = ("sunday", "monday", "tuesday", "wednesday", "thursday", "friday", "saturday")

LOG_STREAM = sys.stdout


def log(level, msg):
    print(f"<{level}>btrfs-snapshot: {msg}", file=LOG_STREAM, flush=True)


class UsageError(Exception):
    pass


class ParseError(Exception):
    pass


# ---- policy --------------------------------------------------------------------------
def parse_policy(text):
    """'72h 7d 4w 3m' -> {'h': 72, 'd': 7, 'w': 4, 'm': 3}; '*' -> 'all'.

    Same tokens as btrbk's preserve matrix (case-insensitive unit, each unit at
    most once); commas are accepted as separators too. An empty policy ("keep
    only the latest") is refused: it is never what a caller of this tool means.
    """
    tokens = [t for t in re.split(r"[\s,]+", text.strip()) if t]
    if not tokens:
        raise ValueError("empty retention policy")
    policy = {}
    for tok in tokens:
        m = POLICY_TOKEN.fullmatch(tok)
        if not m:
            raise ValueError(f"bad retention token {tok!r} (expected <N>h, <N>d, <N>w, <N>m or <N>y)")
        unit = m.group(2).lower()
        if unit in policy:
            raise ValueError(f"retention unit {unit!r} given twice")
        policy[unit] = "all" if m.group(1) == "*" else int(m.group(1))
    return policy


def format_policy(policy):
    return " ".join(f"{'*' if policy[u] == 'all' else policy[u]}{u}" for u in POLICY_UNITS if u in policy)


def policy_max_kept(policy):
    """Upper bound of snapshots the policy keeps (excluding future-dated ones),
    or None if a unit is '*'."""
    total = 1   # the latest
    for u in POLICY_UNITS:
        n = policy.get(u)
        if n == "all":
            return None
        if n:
            total += n + 1   # buckets 0..N
    return total


# ---- time ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
def local_tz(name=None):
    """The zone used for names and retention: --tz, else $TZ, else /etc/localtime."""
    if name:
        return zoneinfo.ZoneInfo(name)
    env = os.environ.get("TZ", "").lstrip(":")
    if env:
        try:
            if env.startswith("/"):
                with open(env, "rb") as f:
                    return zoneinfo.ZoneInfo.from_file(f, key=env)
            return zoneinfo.ZoneInfo(env)
        except (OSError, ValueError, zoneinfo.ZoneInfoNotFoundError):
            pass
    try:
        with open("/etc/localtime", "rb") as f:
            return zoneinfo.ZoneInfo.from_file(f, key="localtime")
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return dt.timezone.utc


def wall_to_epoch(wall, tz):
    """Local wall time -> epoch: an ambiguous time (DST end) maps to the EARLIER
    instant, a non-existent one (DST start) to the instant one DST shift later
    (zoneinfo fold=0). That is Perl's timelocal() (btrbk) for every zone with
    whole-hour DST shifts. Not for Australia/Lord_Howe (30-minute shift): there
    Time::Local 1.35 maps e.g. 01:38 on DST end to 14:08 UTC, an instant that
    localtime() shows as 01:08, not 01:38; fold=0 gives 14:38 UTC, which does
    show as 01:38, so we keep it and accept that plan() may pick a different
    first-of-hour/first-weekly snapshot than btrbk in that half hour."""
    return int(wall.replace(tzinfo=tz, fold=0).timestamp())


def epoch_to_wall(epoch, tz):
    return dt.datetime.fromtimestamp(epoch, tz).replace(tzinfo=None)


def perl_int_div(a, b):
    """Perl's int(a / b) for b > 0: truncates toward zero (matters for future dates)."""
    q = abs(a) // b
    return q if a >= 0 else -q


def snapshot_name(name, wall, seq=0):
    return f"{name}.{wall.strftime(TS_FORMAT)}" + (f"_{seq}" if seq else "")


def name_regex(name):
    return re.compile(re.escape(name) + r"\.([0-9]{8}T[0-9]{4})(?:_([0-9]+))?")


def parse_snapshot_name(name, entry):
    """'home.20261013T1400_1' -> (wall datetime, 1), or None if not ours / invalid date."""
    m = name_regex(name).fullmatch(entry)
    if not m:
        return None
    try:
        wall = dt.datetime.strptime(m.group(1), TS_FORMAT)
    except ValueError:
        return None
    return wall, int(m.group(2) or 0)


# ---- retention (pure) ----------------------------------------------------------------
@dataclasses.dataclass
class Snap:
    entry: str                 # directory entry, e.g. home.20261013T1400
    wall: dt.datetime          # local wall time from the name (naive)
    seq: int = 0               # collision suffix _N
    path: str = ""
    uuid: str = ""
    cgen: int = 0              # Gen at creation
    virtual: bool = False      # dry run: the snapshot this run would create


@dataclasses.dataclass
class Decision:
    snap: Snap
    action: str                # "keep" | "delete"
    reason: str


def plan(snaps, now, policy, tz, *, hod=0, dow=0):
    """Retention of btrbk 0.32.7 schedule() (snapshot_preserve_min latest,
    preserve_date_in_future as in its snapshot deletion). Identical decisions
    and reasons in every zone with whole-hour DST shifts; in Australia/Lord_Howe
    names in the repeated half hour of the DST end are read differently from
    btrbk (see wall_to_epoch()).

    snaps: iterable of Snap (only .wall, .seq used); now: epoch seconds or an
    aware datetime; policy: from parse_policy(); tz: zone of the names.
    Returns [Decision] sorted oldest first; the reason is btrbk's (the last
    rule that matched wins, as in btrbk's output).
    """
    if isinstance(now, dt.datetime):
        now = now.timestamp()
    now_wall = epoch_to_wall(now, tz)
    # btrbk: timegm() of the local hour fields, i.e. DST-blind wall-clock hours.
    now_h = now_wall.replace(minute=0, second=0, microsecond=0)
    dow_name = DAY_NAMES[dow]

    items = []
    for s in snaps:
        epoch = wall_to_epoch(s.wall, tz)
        tm = epoch_to_wall(epoch, tz)          # localtime(): normalizes non-existent times
        wday = (tm.weekday() + 1) % 7          # Perl: Sunday = 0
        hfh = tm.hour - hod                    # hours from start of day
        dfe = wday - dow                       # days from start of week
        if hfh < 0:
            hfh += 24
            dfe -= 1
        if dfe < 0:
            dfe += 7
        month_corr, year_corr = tm.month - 1, tm.year
        if tm.day <= dfe:
            # The month (and year) starts on its first preserve_day_of_week.
            month_corr -= 1
            if month_corr < 0:
                month_corr, year_corr = 11, year_corr - 1
        dh = int((now_h - tm.replace(minute=0, second=0)).total_seconds()) // 3600
        dd = perl_int_div(dh + hfh, 24)
        dw = perl_int_div(dd + dfe, 7)
        dy = now_wall.year - year_corr
        dm = dy * 12 + (now_wall.month - 1 - month_corr)
        items.append({"snap": s, "key": (epoch, s.seq), "dh": dh, "dd": dd, "dw": dw, "dm": dm, "dy": dy,
                      "hfh": hfh, "dfe": dfe, "year": year_corr, "month": month_corr + 1, "reason": None})
    items.sort(key=lambda i: i["key"])

    def delta_text(i):
        s = (f" {i['dfe']}d" if i["dfe"] else "") + (f" {i['hfh']}h" if i["hfh"] else "")
        return (f"{s} after " if s else " at ") + f"{dow_name} {hod:02d}:00"

    def within(unit, delta):
        n = policy.get(unit)
        return bool(n) and (n == "all" or delta <= n)

    first_hours = {}
    for i in items:
        if i["dh"] < 0:
            i["reason"] = f"preserve forced: {-i['dh']} hours in the future"
        first_hours.setdefault(i["dh"], i)
    if items:
        items[-1]["reason"] = "preserve min: latest"

    first_days = {}
    for k in sorted(first_hours, reverse=True):
        i = first_hours[k]
        if within("h", i["dh"]):
            i["reason"] = f"preserve hourly: first of hour, {i['dh']} hours ago"
        first_days.setdefault(i["dd"], i)
    first_weeks = {}
    for k in sorted(first_days, reverse=True):
        i = first_days[k]
        if within("d", i["dd"]):
            i["reason"] = (f"preserve daily: first of day, {i['dd']} days ago"
                           + (f", {i['hfh']}h after " if i["hfh"] else ", at ") + f"{hod:02d}:00")
        first_weeks.setdefault(i["dw"], i)
    first_months = {}
    for k in sorted(first_weeks, reverse=True):
        i = first_weeks[k]
        if within("w", i["dw"]):
            i["reason"] = f"preserve weekly: {i['dw']} weeks ago," + delta_text(i)
        first_months.setdefault(i["dm"], i)
    first_years = {}
    for k in sorted(first_months, reverse=True):
        i = first_months[k]
        if within("m", i["dm"]):
            i["reason"] = (f"preserve monthly: first weekly of month {i['year']}-{i['month']:02d} "
                           f"({i['dm']} months ago," + delta_text(i) + ")")
        first_years.setdefault(i["dy"], i)
    for k in sorted(first_years, reverse=True):
        i = first_years[k]
        if within("y", i["dy"]):
            i["reason"] = (f"preserve yearly: first weekly of year {i['year']} "
                           f"({i['dy']} years ago," + delta_text(i) + ")")

    out = []
    for i in items:
        if i["reason"]:
            out.append(Decision(i["snap"], "keep", i["reason"]))
        else:
            out.append(Decision(i["snap"], "delete", f"not kept by policy '{format_policy(policy)}'"))
    return out


def newest_of(snaps):
    """Newest by Gen at creation (names can lie after a clock step), then by date."""
    return max(snaps, key=lambda s: (s.cgen, s.wall, s.seq), default=None)


def apply_safeguards(decisions, now, tz, pins):
    """Turn policy deletions into keeps for the 3 newest (by generation and by
    name), anything younger than 24 h, and pinned paths."""
    snaps = [d.snap for d in decisions]
    protected = set()
    for key in (lambda s: (s.cgen, s.wall, s.seq), lambda s: (wall_to_epoch(s.wall, tz), s.seq)):
        protected.update(id(s) for s in sorted(snaps, key=key, reverse=True)[:KEEP_NEWEST])
    for d in decisions:
        if d.action != "delete":
            continue
        s = d.snap
        if id(s) in protected:
            d.action, d.reason = "keep", f"safeguard: one of the {KEEP_NEWEST} newest ({d.reason})"
        elif now - wall_to_epoch(s.wall, tz) < MIN_AGE:
            d.action, d.reason = "keep", f"safeguard: younger than 24 h ({d.reason})"
        elif is_pinned(s.path, pins):
            d.action, d.reason = "keep", f"safeguard: pinned ({d.reason})"
    return decisions


def is_pinned(path, pins):
    if not path or not pins:
        return False
    return os.path.normpath(path) in pins or os.path.realpath(path) in pins


def mass_delete_limit(owned):
    return max(MASS_DELETE_MIN, owned * MASS_DELETE_PERCENT // 100)


def is_mass_delete(count, owned):
    """More than max(5, 10% of owned)."""
    return count > MASS_DELETE_MIN and count * 100 > owned * MASS_DELETE_PERCENT


def unexplained_deletions(decisions, snaps, policy, tz, pins, since, ref=None):
    """The deletions in DECISIONS (made now) that time passing does not explain.

    A deletion is explained when the snapshot was kept at SINCE (the last
    successful run: policy and safeguards replayed over the snapshots named up
    to then) and is due at REF (the trusted current time; None = the time the
    decisions were made). After the machine was off, whole days of hourly
    snapshots age out at once and all of them are explained, so the circuit
    breaker does not trip on them; a shrunken policy dooms snapshots that were
    already due at SINCE, and a clock that jumped ahead of REF dooms snapshots
    not yet due at REF - those stay unexplained. SINCE None: nothing is
    explained (no successful run recorded)."""
    doomed = [d for d in decisions if d.action == "delete" and not d.snap.virtual]
    if since is None or not doomed:
        return doomed
    real = [s for s in snaps if not s.virtual]
    before = [s for s in real if wall_to_epoch(s.wall, tz) <= since + CLOCK_SLACK]
    kept_then = {id(d.snap) for d in apply_safeguards(plan(before, since, policy, tz), since, tz, pins)
                 if d.action == "keep"}
    if ref is None:
        due_now = {id(d.snap) for d in doomed}
    else:
        due_now = {id(d.snap) for d in apply_safeguards(plan(real, ref, policy, tz), ref, tz, pins)
                   if d.action == "delete"}
    return [d for d in doomed if not (id(d.snap) in kept_then and id(d.snap) in due_now)]


# ---- btrfs ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SHOW_LABELS = {"UUID": "uuid", "Parent UUID": "parent_uuid", "Generation": "generation",
               "Gen at creation": "cgen"}


def parse_subvolume_show(text):
    """The four fields we use from `btrfs subvolume show`; ParseError on anything odd.

    Label lines are indented; the list after "Snapshot(s):" holds paths that are
    not ours to interpret, so parsing stops there.
    """
    found = {}
    for line in text.splitlines():
        if line.strip().startswith("Snapshot(s):"):
            break
        m = re.match(r"^\s+([^:\t]+?):\s*(.*?)\s*$", line)
        if not m or m.group(1) not in SHOW_LABELS:
            continue
        key = SHOW_LABELS[m.group(1)]
        if key in found:
            raise ParseError(f"label {m.group(1)!r} appears twice")
        found[key] = m.group(2)
    missing = [label for label, key in SHOW_LABELS.items() if key not in found]
    if missing:
        raise ParseError("missing " + ", ".join(missing))
    if not UUID_RE.fullmatch(found["uuid"]):
        raise ParseError(f"bad UUID {found['uuid']!r}")
    if found["parent_uuid"] != "-" and not UUID_RE.fullmatch(found["parent_uuid"]):
        raise ParseError(f"bad Parent UUID {found['parent_uuid']!r}")
    for key in ("generation", "cgen"):
        if not found[key].isdigit():
            raise ParseError(f"bad {key} {found[key]!r}")
    return {"uuid": found["uuid"], "parent_uuid": None if found["parent_uuid"] == "-" else found["parent_uuid"],
            "generation": int(found["generation"]), "cgen": int(found["cgen"])}


class Host:
    """Everything that touches the system; tests replace it."""

    def run(self, argv):
        env = dict(os.environ, LC_ALL="C")
        try:
            p = subprocess.run(argv, check=False, capture_output=True, text=True, env=env, timeout=CMD_TIMEOUT)
        except FileNotFoundError:
            return 127, "", f"{argv[0]}: not found"
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            return 124, "", f"timed out after {CMD_TIMEOUT}s"
        return p.returncode, p.stdout, p.stderr

    def time(self):
        return time.time()

    def boot(self):
        """(boot id, seconds since boot including suspend), or (None, None) if unknown.

        Real time elapsed between two runs of the same boot, whatever the wall
        clock did meanwhile."""
        try:
            with open(BOOT_ID_FILE) as f:
                boot_id = f.read().strip()
            return boot_id, time.clock_gettime(time.CLOCK_BOOTTIME)
        except (OSError, AttributeError):
            return None, None

    def listdir(self, path):
        return sorted(os.listdir(path))

    def kind(self, path):
        """'subvol' (a real subvolume root), 'symlink', 'missing' or 'other'."""
        try:
            st = os.lstat(path)
        except FileNotFoundError:
            return "missing"
        if stat.S_ISLNK(st.st_mode):
            return "symlink"
        if stat.S_ISDIR(st.st_mode) and st.st_ino == 256:
            return "subvol"
        return "other"

    def mkdir(self, path, mode):
        os.mkdir(path, mode)


class Btrfs:
    def __init__(self, host):
        self.host = host

    def show(self, path):
        rc, out, err = self.host.run(["btrfs", "subvolume", "show", path])
        if rc != 0:
            raise ParseError(f"btrfs subvolume show failed (rc={rc}): {err.strip()}")
        return parse_subvolume_show(out)

    def readonly(self, path):
        rc, out, err = self.host.run(["btrfs", "property", "get", "-ts", path, "ro"])
        if rc != 0:
            raise ParseError(f"btrfs property get failed (rc={rc}): {err.strip()}")
        value = out.strip()
        if value == "ro=true":
            return True
        if value == "ro=false":
            return False
        raise ParseError(f"unexpected property output {value!r}")

    def snapshot(self, source, dest):
        return self.host.run(["btrfs", "subvolume", "snapshot", "-r", source, dest])

    def delete(self, path):
        return self.host.run(["btrfs", "subvolume", "delete", path])


@dataclasses.dataclass
class Scan:
    owned: list
    ignored: list              # (path, reason): not ours, never touched
    unverifiable: list         # (path, reason): could be ours, blocks deletions


def scan_snapshots(host, btrfs, snapdir, name, source_uuid):
    """Owned snapshots of one source. source_uuid None = skip the parent check (latest)."""
    owned, ignored, unverifiable = [], [], []
    try:
        entries = host.listdir(snapdir)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return Scan(owned, ignored, unverifiable)
    except OSError as e:
        raise ParseError(f"cannot list {snapdir}: {e.strerror}") from None
    pattern = name_regex(name)
    for entry in entries:
        if not pattern.fullmatch(entry):
            continue
        path = os.path.join(snapdir, entry)
        parsed = parse_snapshot_name(name, entry)
        if parsed is None:
            ignored.append((path, "invalid date in the name"))
            continue
        kind = host.kind(path)
        if kind != "subvol":
            ignored.append((path, "not a subvolume" if kind != "symlink" else "symlink"))
            continue
        try:
            info = btrfs.show(path)
            ro = btrfs.readonly(path)
        except ParseError as e:
            unverifiable.append((path, str(e)))
            continue
        if not ro:
            ignored.append((path, "not read-only"))
            continue
        if source_uuid is not None and info["parent_uuid"] != source_uuid:
            ignored.append((path, f"snapshot of another subvolume (Parent UUID {info['parent_uuid'] or '-'})"))
            continue
        owned.append(Snap(entry=entry, wall=parsed[0], seq=parsed[1], path=path,
                          uuid=info["uuid"], cgen=info["cgen"]))
    return Scan(owned, ignored, unverifiable)


# ---- run context -----------------------------------------------------------------------
@dataclasses.dataclass
class Source:
    name: str
    path: str
    policy: dict
    policy_text: str


def parse_source(text, need_policy=True):
    if "=" not in text:
        raise UsageError(f"--source {text!r}: expected NAME=PATH:POLICY")
    name, rest = text.split("=", 1)
    if not NAME_RE.fullmatch(name):
        raise UsageError(f"--source {text!r}: NAME must match [A-Za-z0-9._-]+")
    if ":" in rest:
        path, policy_text = rest.rsplit(":", 1)
    elif need_policy:
        raise UsageError(f"--source {text!r}: missing :POLICY")
    else:
        path, policy_text = rest, ""
    if not path.startswith("/"):
        raise UsageError(f"--source {text!r}: PATH must be absolute")
    policy = {}
    if need_policy or policy_text.strip():
        try:
            policy = parse_policy(policy_text)
        except ValueError as e:
            raise UsageError(f"--source {text!r}: {e}") from None
    return Source(name, os.path.normpath(path), policy, policy_text.strip())


@dataclasses.dataclass
class LastRun:
    epoch: int
    boot_id: str = None        # None: written without (or by an older format)
    boottime: float = None


def parse_state(text):
    """'EPOCH [BOOT_ID BOOTTIME]' -> LastRun, or None if malformed."""
    parts = text.split()
    if not parts or not parts[0].isdigit():
        return None
    if len(parts) == 1:
        return LastRun(int(parts[0]))
    if len(parts) != 3 or not UUID_RE.fullmatch(parts[1]):
        return None
    try:
        boottime = float(parts[2])
    except ValueError:
        return None
    return LastRun(int(parts[0]), parts[1], boottime)


class Run:
    """State of one snapshot/prune/list invocation."""

    def __init__(self, args, host):
        self.args = args
        self.host = host
        self.btrfs = Btrfs(host)
        self.tz = local_tz(args.tz)
        self.now = host.time()
        self.boot_id, self.boottime = host.boot()
        self.snapdir = os.path.normpath(args.snapdir)
        self._ntp = None
        self._pins = None
        self._last_run = None

    # Clock guards -----------------------------------------------------------
    def ntp_problem(self):
        if self.args.assume_clock_ok:
            return None
        if self._ntp is None:
            rc, out, _ = self.host.run(["timedatectl", "show", "-p", "NTPSynchronized", "--value"])
            self._ntp = (rc == 0 and out.strip() == "yes")
        return None if self._ntp else "the system clock is not NTP-synchronized (timedatectl NTPSynchronized != yes)"

    def state_path(self):
        return os.path.join(self.args.state_dir, STATE_FILE)

    def last_run(self):
        """(LastRun or None, problem or None)."""
        if self._last_run is None:
            try:
                with open(self.state_path()) as f:
                    text = f.read().strip()
                record = parse_state(text)
                self._last_run = (record, None) if record else \
                    (None, f"unreadable state {self.state_path()} ({text[:60]!r}); remove it if the clock is right")
            except FileNotFoundError:
                self._last_run = (None, None)
            except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError) as e:
                self._last_run = (None, f"cannot read {self.state_path()}: {e}")
        return self._last_run

    def behind_last_run(self):
        """Why the clock is not to be trusted against the state file (None = fine)."""
        last, problem = self.last_run()
        if problem:
            return problem
        if last is not None and self.now < last.epoch - CLOCK_SLACK:
            return (f"the clock ({fmt_epoch(self.now, self.tz)}) is behind the last successful run "
                    f"({fmt_epoch(last.epoch, self.tz)}); if the clock is right, remove {self.state_path()}")
        return None

    def backwards_problem(self, newest):
        """Deletions: the clock runs behind what this tool already recorded."""
        problem = self.behind_last_run()
        if problem:
            return problem
        if newest is not None:
            newest_epoch = wall_to_epoch(newest.wall, self.tz)
            if self.now < newest_epoch - CLOCK_SLACK:
                return (f"the clock ({fmt_epoch(self.now, self.tz)}) is behind the newest snapshot "
                        f"{newest.entry}; if the clock is right, that name came from a clock that ran ahead: "
                        f"check it with '{self.args.list_hint}' and delete it with "
                        f"'btrfs subvolume delete {newest.path or newest.entry}'")
        return None

    def clock_jump(self):
        """Seconds the wall clock advanced beyond the real time elapsed since the
        last successful run, if that run was in this boot (else 0: unknown)."""
        last, problem = self.last_run()
        if problem or last is None or last.boot_id is None or self.boot_id is None or self.boottime is None:
            return 0
        if last.boot_id != self.boot_id or self.boottime < last.boottime:
            return 0
        return max(0, (self.now - last.epoch) - (self.boottime - last.boottime))

    def creation_check(self, newest):
        """(problem, notes) for naming a new snapshot after the clock: problem =
        why not to create it now (a refusal), notes = (level, text) to log if it
        is created."""
        problem = self.behind_last_run()
        if problem:
            return problem, []
        synced = self.ntp_problem() is None
        last, _ = self.last_run()
        newest_epoch = wall_to_epoch(newest.wall, self.tz) if newest is not None else None
        notes = []
        if newest_epoch is not None and self.now < newest_epoch - CLOCK_SLACK:
            if not synced:
                return (f"the clock ({fmt_epoch(self.now, self.tz)}) is behind the newest snapshot "
                        f"{newest.entry} and not NTP-synchronized; if the clock is right, that name came "
                        f"from a clock that ran ahead: check it with '{self.args.list_hint}' and delete it "
                        f"with 'btrfs subvolume delete {newest.path}'"), []
            notes.append((4, f"the newest snapshot {newest.entry} is dated in the future (named while the clock "
                         f"ran ahead?); it is kept until its date passes - if the name is wrong, delete it "
                         f"with 'btrfs subvolume delete {newest.path}'"))
        if not synced:
            jump = self.clock_jump()
            if jump > CLOCK_SLACK:
                return (f"the clock is not NTP-synchronized and moved {fmt_span(jump)} further than the time "
                        f"elapsed since the last successful run ({fmt_epoch(last.epoch, self.tz)}, same boot)"), []
            refs = [e for e in (newest_epoch, last.epoch if last else None) if e is not None]
            if (refs and self.now > max(refs) + SAFETY_NET
                    and self.boottime is not None and self.boottime < NTP_GRACE):
                return (f"the clock ({fmt_epoch(self.now, self.tz)}) is not NTP-synchronized yet, the system "
                        f"booted {fmt_span(self.boottime)} ago, and the clock is more than 24 h ahead of the "
                        f"newest snapshot and the last successful run; not freezing a possibly wrong clock "
                        f"into a name - the next run catches up"), []
            notes.append((5, "the clock is not NTP-synchronized; the name uses the unverified time"))
        return None, notes

    def pins(self):
        """(set of pinned paths, problem or None)."""
        if self._pins is None:
            pins = set()
            try:
                with open(self.args.pin_file) as f:
                    for raw in f:
                        line = raw.strip()
                        if line:
                            pins.add(os.path.normpath(line))
                            pins.add(os.path.realpath(line))
                self._pins = (pins, None)
            except FileNotFoundError:
                self._pins = (pins, None)
            except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError) as e:
                self._pins = (pins, f"cannot read the pin file {self.args.pin_file}: {e}")
        return self._pins

    def record_success(self):
        """Store this run as the last successful one - only with a trusted clock,
        and never moving the record backwards (it guards against a clock that
        later runs behind)."""
        if self.ntp_problem():
            log(6, "the clock is not NTP-synchronized; this run is not recorded as the last successful one")
            return
        last, problem = self.last_run()
        if problem:
            log(4, f"not recording this run: {problem}")
            return
        if last is not None and self.now < last.epoch:
            if self.now < last.epoch - CLOCK_SLACK:
                log(4, f"the clock ({fmt_epoch(self.now, self.tz)}) is behind the last successful run "
                       f"({fmt_epoch(last.epoch, self.tz)}); keeping that record - if the clock is right, "
                       f"remove {self.state_path()}")
            return
        line = str(int(self.now))
        if self.boot_id and UUID_RE.fullmatch(self.boot_id) and self.boottime is not None:
            line += f" {self.boot_id} {self.boottime:.3f}"
        os.makedirs(self.args.state_dir, mode=0o755, exist_ok=True)
        path = self.state_path()
        tmp = f"{path}.tmp.{os.getpid()}"
        with open(tmp, "w") as f:
            f.write(line + "\n")
            f.flush()
            os.fsync(f.fileno())
        os.replace(tmp, path)
        self._last_run = (parse_state(line), None)

    # Per source ---------------------------------------------------------------
    def source_info(self, src):
        kind = self.host.kind(src.path)
        if kind != "subvol":
            raise ParseError(f"{src.path} is not a subvolume ({kind})")
        return self.btrfs.show(src.path)

    def decide(self, src, scan, extra=()):
        """Decisions after policy and safeguards, the deletion candidates, and
        why deleting is refused (None = allowed)."""
        snaps = list(scan.owned) + list(extra)
        pins, pin_problem = self.pins()
        decisions = apply_safeguards(plan(snaps, self.now, src.policy, self.tz), self.now, self.tz, pins)
        doomed = [d for d in decisions if d.action == "delete" and not d.snap.virtual]
        refusal = None
        if doomed:
            # A dry run counts the snapshot it would create, like the real run will.
            newest = newest_of(snaps)
            if scan.unverifiable:
                path, why = scan.unverifiable[0]
                refusal = (f"{len(scan.unverifiable)} entr{'y' if len(scan.unverifiable) == 1 else 'ies'} "
                           f"could not be verified ({path}: {why})")
            elif pin_problem:
                refusal = pin_problem
            else:
                refusal = self.backwards_problem(newest) or self.ntp_problem()
            owned = len(snaps)
            if refusal is None and not self.args.allow_mass_delete:
                refusal = self.breaker(src, decisions, snaps, pins, doomed, owned)
        return decisions, doomed, refusal

    def breaker(self, src, decisions, snaps, pins, doomed, owned):
        last, _ = self.last_run()
        since = last.epoch if last is not None else None
        jump = self.clock_jump()
        ref = self.now - jump if jump > CLOCK_SLACK else None
        unexplained = unexplained_deletions(decisions, snaps, src.policy, self.tz, pins, since, ref)
        if not is_mass_delete(len(unexplained), owned):
            return None
        if since is None:
            why = "no successful run is recorded, so none of them is explained by the time passed"
        else:
            why = (f"{len(unexplained)} of them not explained by the time passed since the last successful "
                   f"run ({fmt_epoch(since, self.tz)})")
            if ref is not None:
                why += (f"; the clock moved {fmt_span(jump)} further than the time elapsed since then "
                        f"(same boot)")
            else:
                why += "; was the policy shortened?"
        return (f"circuit breaker: {len(doomed)} of {owned} snapshots would be deleted, {why} "
                f"(limit {mass_delete_limit(owned)}); check with '{self.args.list_hint}', "
                f"then delete them with '{self.args.confirm_hint}'")


def fmt_span(seconds):
    seconds = int(seconds)
    if seconds >= 2 * 86400:
        return f"{seconds // 86400} days"
    if seconds >= 2 * 3600:
        return f"{seconds // 3600} h"
    return f"{seconds // 60} min"


def fmt_epoch(epoch, tz):
    return dt.datetime.fromtimestamp(epoch, tz).strftime("%Y-%m-%d %H:%M %Z")


@dataclasses.dataclass
class Result:
    name: str
    status: int = EXIT_OK
    created: str = ""
    deleted: int = 0
    kept: int = 0
    note: str = ""

    def worse(self, status):
        # error beats refusal beats ok
        rank = {EXIT_OK: 0, EXIT_REFUSED: 1, EXIT_ERROR: 2}
        if rank[status] > rank[self.status]:
            self.status = status


def process_source(run, src, create):
    args = run.args
    res = Result(src.name)
    dry = args.dry_run
    would = "would " if dry else ""
    try:
        info = run.source_info(src)
    except ParseError as e:
        log(3, f"{src.name}: cannot read source {src.path}: {e}; nothing done")
        res.worse(EXIT_ERROR)
        res.note = "source unreadable"
        return res
    try:
        scan = scan_snapshots(run.host, run.btrfs, run.snapdir, src.name, info["uuid"])
    except ParseError as e:
        log(3, f"{src.name}: {e}; nothing done")
        res.worse(EXIT_ERROR)
        return res
    for path, why in scan.unverifiable:
        log(4, f"{src.name}: cannot verify {path}: {why}")
    newest = newest_of(scan.owned)
    extra = []

    if create:
        reason = None
        if args.force:
            reason = "forced"
        elif newest is None:
            reason = "no snapshot yet"
        elif info["generation"] > newest.cgen:
            reason = f"changed (generation {info['generation']} > {newest.cgen} of {newest.entry})"
        elif run.now - wall_to_epoch(newest.wall, run.tz) > SAFETY_NET:
            reason = f"newest snapshot {newest.entry} is older than 24 h"
        elif run.now < wall_to_epoch(newest.wall, run.tz) - CLOCK_SLACK and run.ntp_problem() is None:
            # Named while the clock ran ahead. A correctly named snapshot becomes
            # the newest by generation, which lets deletions resume.
            reason = f"newest snapshot {newest.entry} is dated in the future"
        if reason is None:
            log(6, f"{src.name}: unchanged since {newest.entry}; no new snapshot")
        else:
            problem, notes = run.creation_check(newest)
            if problem:
                log(4, f"{src.name}: not creating a snapshot: {problem}")
                res.worse(EXIT_REFUSED)
                res.note = "clock"
            else:
                for level, note in notes:
                    log(level, f"{src.name}: {note}")
                wall = epoch_to_wall(run.now, run.tz).replace(second=0, microsecond=0)
                entry = free_name(run.host, run.snapdir, src.name, wall)
                dest = os.path.join(run.snapdir, entry)
                if dry:
                    log(5, f"{src.name}: would create {dest} ({reason})")
                    # The real snapshot's "Gen at creation" is the running transaction:
                    # above the source's generation and every existing snapshot's.
                    cgen = max([info["generation"]] + [s.cgen for s in scan.owned]) + 1
                    extra.append(Snap(entry=entry, wall=wall, seq=parse_snapshot_name(src.name, entry)[1],
                                      path=dest, cgen=cgen, virtual=True))
                    res.created = entry
                elif create_snapshot(run, src, info, dest, entry, reason):
                    res.created = entry
                    try:
                        scan = scan_snapshots(run.host, run.btrfs, run.snapdir, src.name, info["uuid"])
                    except ParseError as e:
                        log(3, f"{src.name}: {e}; no deletions")
                        res.worse(EXIT_ERROR)
                        return res
                    if not any(s.entry == entry for s in scan.owned):
                        log(3, f"{src.name}: created {dest} but it does not pass the ownership check")
                        res.worse(EXIT_ERROR)
                else:
                    res.worse(EXIT_ERROR)

    decisions, doomed, refusal = run.decide(src, scan, extra)
    res.kept = sum(1 for d in decisions if d.action == "keep" and not d.snap.virtual)
    if not doomed:
        return res
    if refusal:
        log(4, f"{src.name}: {len(doomed)} deletion(s) blocked: {refusal}")
        for d in doomed:
            log(6, f"{src.name}: blocked: {d.snap.entry} ({d.reason})")
        res.worse(EXIT_REFUSED)
        res.kept += len(doomed)
        return res
    if dry:
        for d in doomed:
            log(5, f"{src.name}: {would}delete {d.snap.path} ({d.reason})")
        res.deleted = len(doomed)
        return res

    # Right before deleting: list again, decide again, and keep only what both
    # passes agree on (same UUID); then re-check each one just before its delete.
    run._pins = None
    try:
        fresh = scan_snapshots(run.host, run.btrfs, run.snapdir, src.name, info["uuid"])
    except ParseError as e:
        log(3, f"{src.name}: {e}; no deletions")
        res.worse(EXIT_ERROR)
        return res
    _, fresh_doomed, refusal = run.decide(src, fresh)
    if refusal:
        log(4, f"{src.name}: deletions blocked on re-check: {refusal}")
        res.worse(EXIT_REFUSED)
        res.kept += len(doomed)
        return res
    fresh_uuids = {d.snap.uuid for d in fresh_doomed}
    for d in doomed:
        s = d.snap
        if s.uuid not in fresh_uuids:
            log(5, f"{src.name}: {s.entry} is no longer due after re-listing; kept")
            res.kept += 1
            continue
        # Pins are re-read before every deletion; a pin is protection, not a fault.
        run._pins = None
        if is_pinned(s.path, run.pins()[0]):
            log(5, f"{src.name}: {s.entry} was pinned meanwhile; kept")
            res.kept += 1
            continue
        problem = recheck(run, s, info["uuid"])
        if problem:
            log(4, f"{src.name}: not deleting {s.path}: {problem}")
            res.worse(EXIT_REFUSED)
            res.kept += 1
            continue
        rc, _, err = run.btrfs.delete(s.path)
        if rc != 0:
            log(3, f"{src.name}: deleting {s.path} failed (rc={rc}): {err.strip()}; stopping deletions")
            res.worse(EXIT_ERROR)
            break
        log(5, f"{src.name}: deleted {s.path} ({d.reason})")
        res.deleted += 1
    return res


def recheck(run, snap, source_uuid):
    """Why SNAP must not be deleted after all (None = go ahead)."""
    problem = run.pins()[1]
    if problem:
        return problem
    if run.host.kind(snap.path) != "subvol":
        return "no longer a subvolume"
    try:
        info = run.btrfs.show(snap.path)
        ro = run.btrfs.readonly(snap.path)
    except ParseError as e:
        return str(e)
    if info["uuid"] != snap.uuid:
        return f"UUID changed ({snap.uuid} -> {info['uuid']})"
    if info["parent_uuid"] != source_uuid:
        return "Parent UUID changed"
    if not ro:
        return "no longer read-only"
    return None


def free_name(host, snapdir, name, wall):
    """NAME.TS, or NAME.TS_N with N = highest present + 1 (btrbk)."""
    base = snapshot_name(name, wall)
    pattern = re.compile(re.escape(base) + r"(?:_([0-9]+))?")
    try:
        entries = host.listdir(snapdir)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        entries = []
    taken = [int(m.group(1) or 0) for m in map(pattern.fullmatch, entries) if m]
    return base if not taken else f"{base}_{max(taken) + 1}"


def create_snapshot(run, src, info, dest, entry, reason):
    if run.host.kind(run.snapdir) == "missing":
        try:
            run.host.mkdir(run.snapdir, 0o700)
            log(5, f"created the snapshot directory {run.snapdir}")
        except OSError as e:
            log(3, f"cannot create {run.snapdir}: {e.strerror}")
            return False
    rc, _, err = run.btrfs.snapshot(src.path, dest)
    if rc != 0:
        log(3, f"{src.name}: creating {dest} failed (rc={rc}): {err.strip()}")
        return False
    log(5, f"{src.name}: created {entry} ({reason})")
    return True


# ---- commands ------------------------------------------------------------------------
def take_lock(args):
    """Returns the open fd (close it to unlock), -1 if no lock is wanted, or None if busy."""
    if args.no_lock or args.dry_run:
        return -1
    fd = os.open(args.lock, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_CLOEXEC | os.O_NOFOLLOW, 0o600)
    try:
        fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
    except BlockingIOError:
        os.close(fd)
        return None
    return fd


def check_snapdir(host, snapdir):
    # A missing directory just holds no snapshots yet (snapshot creates it).
    if host.kind(snapdir) == "symlink":
        raise UsageError(f"--snapdir {snapdir} is a symlink")


def cmd_snapshot_or_prune(args, host, create):
    check_snapdir(host, args.snapdir)
    fd = take_lock(args)
    if fd is None:
        log(5, f"another run holds {args.lock}; skipped")
        return EXIT_BUSY
    try:
        return snapshot_or_prune(args, host, create)
    finally:
        if fd >= 0:
            os.close(fd)


def snapshot_or_prune(args, host, create):
    run = Run(args, host)
    results = [process_source(run, src, create) for src in args.sources]
    status = EXIT_OK
    for r in results:
        if r.status == EXIT_ERROR or (r.status == EXIT_REFUSED and status == EXIT_OK):
            status = r.status
    parts = []
    for r in results:
        what = f"created {r.created}" if r.created else ("no new snapshot" if create else "")
        bits = [b for b in (what, f"deleted {r.deleted}", f"kept {r.kept}", r.note) if b]
        parts.append(f"{r.name}: " + ", ".join(bits))
    word = {EXIT_OK: "ok", EXIT_REFUSED: "blocked by a safety check", EXIT_ERROR: "error"}[status]
    log(5 if status == EXIT_OK else 4 if status == EXIT_REFUSED else 3,
        f"{'dry run ' if args.dry_run else ''}{'snapshot' if create else 'prune'} summary: "
        + "; ".join(parts) + f"; result: {word}")
    if status == EXIT_OK and not args.dry_run:
        try:
            run.record_success()
        except OSError as e:
            log(4, f"cannot record the run in {args.state_dir}: {e.strerror}")
    return status


def cmd_list(args, host):
    run = Run(args, host)
    out = []
    status = EXIT_OK
    for src in args.sources:
        entry = {"name": src.name, "source": src.path, "policy": format_policy(src.policy)}
        try:
            info = run.source_info(src)
        except ParseError as e:
            log(3, f"{src.name}: cannot read source {src.path}: {e}")
            entry["error"] = str(e)
            out.append(entry)
            status = EXIT_ERROR
            continue
        try:
            scan = scan_snapshots(host, run.btrfs, run.snapdir, src.name, info["uuid"])
        except ParseError as e:
            log(3, f"{src.name}: {e}")
            entry["error"] = str(e)
            out.append(entry)
            status = EXIT_ERROR
            continue
        decisions, doomed, refusal = run.decide(src, scan)
        newest = newest_of(scan.owned)
        entry.update({
            "uuid": info["uuid"], "generation": info["generation"],
            "changed": newest is None or info["generation"] > newest.cgen,
            "snapshots": [{"path": d.snap.path, "entry": d.snap.entry,
                           "time": d.snap.wall.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M"), "uuid": d.snap.uuid,
                           "gen_at_creation": d.snap.cgen, "action": d.action, "reason": d.reason}
                          for d in decisions],
            "ignored": [{"path": p, "reason": r} for p, r in scan.ignored],
            "unverifiable": [{"path": p, "reason": r} for p, r in scan.unverifiable],
            "deletions_blocked": refusal,
        })
        out.append(entry)
    if args.json:
        json.dump({"snapdir": run.snapdir, "now": int(run.now), "sources": out}, sys.stdout, indent=2)
        print()
        return status
    for e in out:
        if "error" in e:
            print(f"{e['name']}: {e['source']}: ERROR {e['error']}")
            continue
        print(f"{e['name']}: {e['source']} (generation {e['generation']}, "
              f"{'changed' if e['changed'] else 'unchanged'} since the newest snapshot), policy '{e['policy']}'")
        for s in e["snapshots"]:
            print(f"  {s['action'].upper():7} {s['entry']:<32} {s['reason']}")
        for s in e["ignored"]:
            print(f"  {'IGNORED':7} {os.path.basename(s['path']):<32} {s['reason']}")
        for s in e["unverifiable"]:
            print(f"  {'UNKNOWN':7} {os.path.basename(s['path']):<32} {s['reason']}")
        n_del = sum(1 for s in e["snapshots"] if s["action"] == "delete")
        print(f"  {len(e['snapshots'])} snapshot(s), {n_del} due for deletion"
              + (f"; deletions would be blocked: {e['deletions_blocked']}" if e["deletions_blocked"] else ""))
    return status


def cmd_latest(args, host):
    check_snapdir(host, args.snapdir)
    btrfs = Btrfs(host)
    source_uuid = None
    if args.source_path:
        try:
            source_uuid = btrfs.show(os.path.normpath(args.source_path))["uuid"]
        except ParseError as e:
            log(3, f"cannot read {args.source_path}: {e}")
            return EXIT_ERROR
    try:
        scan = scan_snapshots(host, btrfs, os.path.normpath(args.snapdir), args.name, source_uuid)
    except ParseError as e:
        log(3, str(e))
        return EXIT_ERROR
    for path, why in scan.unverifiable:
        log(4, f"cannot verify {path}: {why}")
    newest = newest_of(scan.owned)
    if newest is None:
        log(4, f"no snapshot of {args.name} in {args.snapdir}")
        return EXIT_ERROR
    print(newest.path)
    return EXIT_OK


def build_parser():
    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="btrfs-snapshot-core",
                                description="Local btrfs snapshots with btrbk-compatible retention.")
    sub = p.add_subparsers(dest="cmd", required=True)

    def common(sp, policy=True):
        sp.add_argument("--snapdir", required=True, help="directory holding the snapshots")
        sp.add_argument("--source", action="append", required=True, dest="sources", metavar="NAME=PATH:POLICY",
                        help="snapshot name, source subvolume and retention, e.g. home=/mnt/top/home:'72h 7d'")
        sp.add_argument("--tz", help="time zone of the names and the retention (default: local; for tests)")
        sp.add_argument("--pin-file", default=DEFAULT_PIN_FILE, help=f"pinned paths (default {DEFAULT_PIN_FILE})")
        sp.add_argument("--state-dir", default=DEFAULT_STATE_DIR, help=f"default {DEFAULT_STATE_DIR}")
        sp.add_argument("--assume-clock-ok", action="store_true",
                        help="skip the NTP-synchronized check (tests); the other clock guards stay")
        # A caller that builds the --source arguments itself (btrfs-snapshot)
        # names its own runnable commands in the messages instead of ours.
        sp.add_argument("--list-hint", default=DEFAULT_LIST_HINT, metavar="COMMAND",
                        help="command named in messages to review the decisions")
        sp.add_argument("--confirm-hint", default=DEFAULT_CONFIRM_HINT, metavar="COMMAND",
                        help="command named in messages to confirm a mass deletion")

    for name, helptext in (("snapshot", "create changed snapshots, then prune"), ("prune", "prune only")):
        sp = sub.add_parser(name, help=helptext)
        common(sp)
        sp.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true", help="show what would be created/deleted")
        sp.add_argument("--allow-mass-delete", action="store_true", help="disable the circuit breaker")
        sp.add_argument("--lock", default=DEFAULT_LOCK, help=f"lock file (default {DEFAULT_LOCK})")
        sp.add_argument("--no-lock", action="store_true", help="the caller already holds the lock")
        if name == "snapshot":
            sp.add_argument("--force", action="store_true", help="create even if unchanged")
    sp = sub.add_parser("list", help="list snapshots with the retention decision")
    common(sp)
    sp.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")
    sp = sub.add_parser("latest", help="print the newest snapshot of NAME")
    sp.add_argument("--snapdir", required=True)
    sp.add_argument("--name", required=True)
    sp.add_argument("--source-path", help="also require the snapshot's Parent UUID to be this subvolume's UUID")
    return p


def main(argv=None, host=None):
    global LOG_STREAM
    host = host or Host()
    parser = build_parser()
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)
    LOG_STREAM = sys.stderr if args.cmd == "latest" or getattr(args, "json", False) else sys.stdout
    for attr in ("force", "allow_mass_delete", "dry_run", "no_lock"):
        if not hasattr(args, attr):
            setattr(args, attr, False)
    for attr, default in (("list_hint", DEFAULT_LIST_HINT), ("confirm_hint", DEFAULT_CONFIRM_HINT)):
        if not hasattr(args, attr):
            setattr(args, attr, default)
    try:
        if not args.snapdir.startswith("/"):
            raise UsageError("--snapdir must be absolute")
        if args.cmd == "latest":
            if not NAME_RE.fullmatch(args.name):
                raise UsageError("--name must match [A-Za-z0-9._-]+")
            return cmd_latest(args, host)
        if args.tz:
            try:
                zoneinfo.ZoneInfo(args.tz)
            except (zoneinfo.ZoneInfoNotFoundError, ValueError):
                raise UsageError(f"unknown --tz {args.tz!r}") from None
        args.sources = [parse_source(s) for s in args.sources]
        names = [s.name for s in args.sources]
        paths = [s.path for s in args.sources]
        if len(set(names)) != len(names) or len(set(paths)) != len(paths):
            raise UsageError("each --source needs its own NAME and PATH")
        if args.cmd == "list":
            return cmd_list(args, host)
        return cmd_snapshot_or_prune(args, host, create=(args.cmd == "snapshot"))
    except UsageError as e:
        parser.print_usage(sys.stderr)
        print(f"btrfs-snapshot-core: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return EXIT_USAGE


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
